#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# kelvin_ker

#% text
# `kelvin_ker(nu, x)` gives the Kelvin function `ker_nu(x)`, the real part of `exp(-nu*pi*im/2)*bessel_k(nu, x*exp(pi*im/4))`.

#% text
# `kelvin_ker(x)` gives `ker_0(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The definition is DLMF 10.61.2, for `x > 0`; `ker` has a logarithmic singularity at 0 and decays for large `x`.

#% item
# Machine numbers and complex numbers give numerical values at any real order; arbitrary-precision arguments with a non-zero order are not reduced to a number (see Possible issues). A symbolic argument stays.

#% item
# For a negative real `x` the value is the principal one and has an imaginary part; `kelvin_ker(0.0)` is `inf`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
kelvin_ker(1.0)
#% output
#= 0.2867062087283161

#% cell
kelvin_ker(2, 2.5)
#% output
#= 0.18880430952581595

#% cell
[kelvin_ker(2.5), kelvin_ker(-1, 0.5)]
#% output
#= [-0.06968797258904535, 1.52240340653209]

#% text
# Symbolic arguments stay as they are:

#% cell
kelvin_ker(x)
#% output
#= kelvin_ker(x)

#% cell
kelvin_ker(nu, x)
#% output
#= kelvin_ker(nu, x)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A non-integer order, and a complex argument:

#% cell
kelvin_ker(0.5, 2.5)
#% output
#= -0.13273516893743098

#% cell
kelvin_ker(1.0 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= -0.15110610751868647 - 0.4391429301194704im

#% text
# Small and large arguments, and the singularity at 0:

#% cell
[kelvin_ker(1e-10), kelvin_ker(10.0), kelvin_ker(800.0), kelvin_ker(0.0)]
#% output
#= [23.141782445598867, 0.000129466330214806, 7.790931487780324e-248, inf]

#% text
# 30 digits at order 0:

#% cell
n(kelvin_ker(1), 30)
#% output
#= 0.286706208728316045954197314872301775449

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The definition, at order 0, 2 and a non-integer order:

#% cell
kelvin_ker(1.5) - re(bessel_k(0, 1.5*exp(pi*im/4)))
#% output
#= 0.0

#% cell
kelvin_ker(2, 1.5) - re(exp(-2*pi*im/2)*bessel_k(2, 1.5*exp(pi*im/4)))
#% output
#= 0.0

#% cell
kelvin_ker(0.7, 1.5) - re(exp(-0.7*pi*im/2)*bessel_k(0.7, 1.5*exp(pi*im/4)))
#% output
#= 0.0

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Exact order 0 and exact argument 0 are not evaluated, and the derivative is not given in closed form:

#% cell
kelvin_ker(0)
#% output
#= inf

#% cell
d(kelvin_ker(x), x)
#% output
#= (1/2)*(kelvin_kei(1, x) + kelvin_ker(1, x))*2^(1/2)

#% text
# At arbitrary precision a non-zero order gives an expression with `re` rather than a number, and a very large machine argument stays the real part of Bessel functions:

#% cell
n(kelvin_ker(2, 5/2), 30)
#% output
#= 0.18880430952581467051408141361075255419

#% cell
kelvin_ker(1e5)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `kelvin_kei`, `kelvin_ber`, `kelvin_bei`, `bessel_k`
